WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:03.220
Hi, everyone. My name is Martin Cousins.

2
00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:05.299
I'm going to be your host for this training session

3
00:00:05.299 --> 00:00:07.620
into Introduction to AI in Unreal Engine

4
00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:10.820
for Programmers for Unreal 5.5.

5
00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:12.339
So what are we going to cover today?

6
00:00:12.339 --> 00:00:15.300
We've got looking into the AI Controller, Behavior Tree,

7
00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:17.820
Blackboard, Navigation Mesh, EQS,

8
00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:20.620
or the Environmental Query System, AI Perception,

9
00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:22.820
and then a little bit about some of the useful plugins

10
00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:25.620
that can be useful tools in your belt

11
00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:26.899
if you're working with AI stuff.

12
00:00:26.899 --> 00:00:28.620
So that's smart objects, state trees,

13
00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:31.660
mass entity, and lastly the zone graph.

14
00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:33.420
So first off the AI Controller.

15
00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:36.579
Now this is the third session in a series of five.

16
00:00:36.579 --> 00:00:38.659
So some of these, this initial concept

17
00:00:38.659 --> 00:00:40.340
is going to be a little bit of repetition

18
00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:42.219
from previous sessions that you may have seen,

19
00:00:42.219 --> 00:00:45.060
but just as a kind of like building up the basis again.

20
00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:46.740
So when we're talking about the AI Controller,

21
00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:49.299
this is a controller that is specifically designed

22
00:00:49.299 --> 00:00:52.299
to possess and control AI objects.

23
00:00:52.299 --> 00:00:54.899
It's a non-physical actor that can possess a pawn

24
00:00:54.899 --> 00:00:56.179
like any controller can.

25
00:00:57.140 --> 00:00:59.299
It's got a couple of functions made available to it

26
00:00:59.299 --> 00:01:01.380
through onPossess and onUnPossess,

27
00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.659
meaning the AI Controller can be notified.

28
00:01:03.659 --> 00:01:05.140
And what the AI Controller,

29
00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:07.340
and generally controllers in broad

30
00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:08.219
are kind of meant to represent

31
00:01:08.219 --> 00:01:09.700
is the kind of a will of the thing

32
00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:11.019
that they are controlling.

33
00:01:11.019 --> 00:01:14.420
So in this context, being the AI Controller,

34
00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:17.579
it's kind of represents the will of an AI entity,

35
00:01:17.579 --> 00:01:19.180
an AI actor.

36
00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:20.299
That can be any agent.

37
00:01:20.299 --> 00:01:21.540
Typically it's going to be

38
00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:25.140
some kind of humanoid-esque character, probably,

39
00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:26.579
but really this could be there

40
00:01:26.579 --> 00:01:29.340
to kind of represent any controllable pawn

41
00:01:29.659 --> 00:01:32.219
and represent its kind of logic and thinking.

42
00:01:32.219 --> 00:01:33.700
Whereas the pawn is meant to be there

43
00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:36.219
to represent the actual physical actions

44
00:01:36.219 --> 00:01:38.980
being performed in the world.

45
00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:41.620
As with other controllers,

46
00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:43.420
AI Controllers have a one-to-one relationship

47
00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:44.420
with the pawn.

48
00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:48.900
If you have something like a hack and slash

49
00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:51.379
where you want kind of mob behavior

50
00:01:51.379 --> 00:01:55.620
and you want 20 enemies being controlled by one manager,

51
00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:59.260
that's not really the kind of fundamental basis

52
00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:00.260
for an AI Controller.

53
00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:03.299
It is intended to have a one controller controls one pawn,

54
00:02:03.299 --> 00:02:05.659
one pawn is controlled by a single controller.

55
00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:08.340
So if you need something else,

56
00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:09.939
you might need to look into

57
00:02:09.939 --> 00:02:11.300
a more bespoke implementation.

58
00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:13.259
The standard intent for an AI Controller

59
00:02:13.259 --> 00:02:15.849
is a one-to-one relationship.

60
00:02:15.849 --> 00:02:17.930
So a couple of notes about the AI Controller.

61
00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:19.490
It only exists on the server.

62
00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:21.810
We've spoken about this before

63
00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:25.370
in our second session on Gameplay Framework,

64
00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:27.930
but essentially the server

65
00:02:27.969 --> 00:02:30.050
is really what houses all the controllers.

66
00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:33.009
For player controllers, each individual client player

67
00:02:33.009 --> 00:02:34.889
will see their own player controller.

68
00:02:34.889 --> 00:02:38.530
But really it is just a general pervading logic.

69
00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:41.169
The server is the authoritative state

70
00:02:41.169 --> 00:02:43.009
and therefore it should be the server

71
00:02:43.009 --> 00:02:45.770
making all the decisions about what the AIs are doing.

72
00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:47.729
And therefore the AI controllers,

73
00:02:47.729 --> 00:02:50.009
which are the brain should exist on the server.

74
00:02:50.009 --> 00:02:52.409
And really all the AI pawns

75
00:02:52.409 --> 00:02:54.330
are there to do on each individual client

76
00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:56.129
is kind of mimics the behavior

77
00:02:56.129 --> 00:02:57.770
that the server is deciding.

78
00:02:58.449 --> 00:02:59.689
You wouldn't want a clash

79
00:02:59.689 --> 00:03:01.250
between different bits of AI logic.

80
00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:03.569
You wouldn't want an AI on the client side

81
00:03:03.569 --> 00:03:04.610
to be thinking one thing

82
00:03:04.610 --> 00:03:06.169
and the AI on the server side

83
00:03:06.169 --> 00:03:07.409
to be thinking another thing.

84
00:03:07.409 --> 00:03:09.530
So all of that logic is centralized on the server

85
00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:13.689
and it's simply the clients kind of parrot the actions

86
00:03:13.689 --> 00:03:17.099
of what the brain decides to do.

87
00:03:17.099 --> 00:03:20.900
So the AI Controller houses kind of four main components.

88
00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:22.580
The behavior tree component,

89
00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:24.939
which inherits from the brain component.

90
00:03:24.939 --> 00:03:28.099
So it's pretty explicit there.

91
00:03:28.099 --> 00:03:30.819
It's kind of the thing that actually does the thinking.

92
00:03:30.819 --> 00:03:31.979
The blackboard component.

93
00:03:31.979 --> 00:03:33.460
So if you think of,

94
00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:35.020
we'll talk more about blackboards in a bit,

95
00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:36.659
but this is essentially the,

96
00:03:36.659 --> 00:03:38.300
close thing is the memory,

97
00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:39.699
the way they're kind of,

98
00:03:39.699 --> 00:03:42.659
the brain kind of hold onto data between frames

99
00:03:42.659 --> 00:03:45.259
across time for use later.

100
00:03:45.259 --> 00:03:47.379
Perception component, pretty self-explanatory.

101
00:03:47.379 --> 00:03:49.500
This is essentially how the AI

102
00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:51.979
can sense things about the world.

103
00:03:51.979 --> 00:03:53.860
We'll go into more detail about that later.

104
00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:56.180
And then lastly, the path following component,

105
00:03:56.180 --> 00:03:59.460
which is how AI agents follow paths

106
00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:02.099
use the navigation system that Unreal provides.

107
00:04:03.939 --> 00:04:05.259
This didn't used to be the case,

108
00:04:05.259 --> 00:04:07.259
but in more recent versions of Unreal,

109
00:04:07.259 --> 00:04:08.819
I don't have the exact point

110
00:04:08.819 --> 00:04:11.740
at which this became a thing to hand,

111
00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:13.819
but in more recent versions of Unreal,

112
00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:17.899
AIs can now kind of initiate the AI logic

113
00:04:17.899 --> 00:04:20.060
as soon as they get possessed.

114
00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:21.259
They didn't used to be able to do this,

115
00:04:21.259 --> 00:04:23.339
but if I just quickly jump to the engine,

116
00:04:23.379 --> 00:04:25.579
that is through,

117
00:04:27.519 --> 00:04:29.439
if we go into an AI controller,

118
00:04:29.439 --> 00:04:30.759
there has a,

119
00:04:30.759 --> 00:04:35.120
there's a bull called start AI logic on possess.

120
00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:36.439
So if that's ticked,

121
00:04:36.439 --> 00:04:41.160
then as soon as that AI controller gets initializes,

122
00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:42.959
as soon as it possesses an entity,

123
00:04:42.959 --> 00:04:44.959
it will start its AI logic

124
00:04:44.959 --> 00:04:48.279
if it has a behavior tree component

125
00:04:48.279 --> 00:04:50.160
already on the controller.

126
00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:52.920
We'll get into more of this later.

127
00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:55.199
Importantly, AI controllers implement

128
00:04:55.199 --> 00:04:57.560
what's called a generic team interface.

129
00:04:57.560 --> 00:04:59.079
This is something that you,

130
00:04:59.079 --> 00:05:02.079
I don't believe you can perceive in blueprint

131
00:05:02.079 --> 00:05:04.040
because it is more of a C plus plus concept.

132
00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:06.399
So if we just quickly jump to the C plus plus,

133
00:05:06.399 --> 00:05:07.240
we can actually see this.

134
00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:15.139
If I just go to ai-controller.h,

135
00:05:15.139 --> 00:05:18.930
generic team, yeah.

136
00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:22.129
And you can see that it implements this interface.

137
00:05:22.129 --> 00:05:25.209
This interface is really a way for controllers

138
00:05:25.209 --> 00:05:26.689
and other entities in the world.

139
00:05:26.689 --> 00:05:30.009
It's Unreal's built-in way for entities

140
00:05:30.009 --> 00:05:31.889
to perceive themselves and each other

141
00:05:31.889 --> 00:05:33.370
as part of a particular team,

142
00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:35.769
as they kind of name would imply.

143
00:05:35.769 --> 00:05:38.930
So this is useful for some of the perception components

144
00:05:38.930 --> 00:05:40.769
we'll get to in a little bit.

145
00:05:40.769 --> 00:05:44.370
It helps basically entities give an idea of like,

146
00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:46.410
should I treat this thing as an enemy?

147
00:05:46.410 --> 00:05:47.610
Should I treat this thing as a friend

148
00:05:47.610 --> 00:05:49.209
or am I neutral to it?

149
00:05:49.209 --> 00:05:53.370
The standard implementation is as soon as two agents

150
00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:56.329
are on different teams, they're automatically enemies.

151
00:05:56.329 --> 00:05:57.889
We'll get into that more in a bit,

152
00:05:57.889 --> 00:06:00.370
but just know that Unreal has a default implementation

153
00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:02.050
of the idea of teams.

154
00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:04.810
Then by default, that's on the AI controller

155
00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:05.930
and it kind of implements it.

156
00:06:05.930 --> 00:06:07.410
I think it just goes to,

157
00:06:07.410 --> 00:06:08.970
I think the default implementation is

158
00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:11.850
it's just the default team, which is like,

159
00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:14.329
I think the teams are represented by a byte

160
00:06:14.329 --> 00:06:17.410
and so they're just team 255, like the maximum.

161
00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:18.649
And you would have to kind of manually

162
00:06:18.649 --> 00:06:20.009
implement that yourselves.

163
00:06:20.009 --> 00:06:24.399
But just again, worth mentioning that the agent,

164
00:06:24.399 --> 00:06:27.920
the interface can be implemented on objects,

165
00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:30.000
but it isn't a blueprint interface.

166
00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:32.120
So none of this functionality is exposed to blueprint

167
00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:34.399
and would have to be kind of driven on C++,

168
00:06:34.399 --> 00:06:35.360
but that is there.

169
00:06:36.839 --> 00:06:38.600
And then, yeah, last of all,

170
00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:39.839
it's kind of just worth mentioning

171
00:06:39.839 --> 00:06:42.040
that we've got a kind of entry function

172
00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:44.920
for selecting possible targets based on our perception.

173
00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:46.240
We have this function,

174
00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:49.120
actors perception updated function,

175
00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:51.279
which sends through an array of any actors

176
00:06:51.279 --> 00:06:52.480
that have been recently perceived.

177
00:06:52.519 --> 00:06:55.560
And then that way the AI can kind of make decisions

178
00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:59.660
about possible targets for its various logic.

179
00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:01.939
So that's the behavior tree.

180
00:07:01.939 --> 00:07:04.180
Next, we have the good kind of going into,

181
00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:06.939
in the broad at least, going into kind of more detail.

182
00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:12.980
As we already mentioned, we have the behavior tree itself.

183
00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:14.379
The behavior tree component

184
00:07:14.379 --> 00:07:17.100
is really what hosts the behavior tree.

185
00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:18.220
So the behavior tree component

186
00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:20.740
is the kind of brain of the AI agent.

187
00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:23.899
It inherits from brain components, as I already mentioned,

188
00:07:23.899 --> 00:07:27.139
and it's actually held on the AI controller.

189
00:07:27.139 --> 00:07:31.100
AI controllers won't have a brain component automatically.

190
00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:32.540
You can either add one

191
00:07:33.459 --> 00:07:38.459
or you can generate one at runtime by calling it on C++.

192
00:07:38.579 --> 00:07:40.379
We'll go into that in a little bit.

193
00:07:40.379 --> 00:07:41.779
You can call a function at runtime

194
00:07:41.779 --> 00:07:44.620
to kind of create a behavior tree component

195
00:07:44.620 --> 00:07:46.019
if one doesn't already exist.

196
00:07:46.019 --> 00:07:49.019
We'll go into more detail about that in a minute.

197
00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:51.819
But a couple of things about the behavior tree component.

198
00:07:51.819 --> 00:07:52.980
It does make an assumption

199
00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:54.939
that the owner is an AI controller.

200
00:07:54.939 --> 00:07:56.420
So we talked about this again,

201
00:07:56.420 --> 00:07:58.060
I think in the second session,

202
00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:01.339
but essentially these two things are tightly linked.

203
00:08:01.339 --> 00:08:03.379
Behavior tree component without an AI controller

204
00:08:03.379 --> 00:08:04.980
doesn't really make sense.

205
00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:07.699
So it does kind of, the behavior tree component

206
00:08:07.699 --> 00:08:10.139
is aware of the AI controller

207
00:08:10.139 --> 00:08:11.939
that should be holding onto it.

208
00:08:11.939 --> 00:08:14.220
It even has functions like getAIOwner,

209
00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:16.180
which really, really strongly implied that, you know,

210
00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:18.980
this component is meant to be held by an AI.

211
00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:23.339
The, yeah, it's a blueprint spawnable component,

212
00:08:23.339 --> 00:08:25.660
so you can add it onto your controllers

213
00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:27.500
if you kind of want that preset up.

214
00:08:27.500 --> 00:08:28.579
As I already mentioned,

215
00:08:28.579 --> 00:08:32.059
that idea of running logic on possess doesn't work

216
00:08:32.059 --> 00:08:35.740
if you haven't pre-added a behavior tree component yourself,

217
00:08:35.740 --> 00:08:37.179
but there are other ways of doing it.

218
00:08:37.179 --> 00:08:40.059
You can run a behavior tree at runtime

219
00:08:40.059 --> 00:08:42.860
and that will automatically add a behavior tree component

220
00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:45.419
if one doesn't already exist to the AI controller.

221
00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:49.220
Yep.

222
00:08:49.220 --> 00:08:53.500
So, as we already said, it exposes,

223
00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:54.580
the behavior tree component itself

224
00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:57.659
exposes what behavior tree you want to use.

225
00:08:57.659 --> 00:08:59.259
One will be created at runtime

226
00:08:59.259 --> 00:09:00.980
if we choose to run a behavior tree,

227
00:09:00.980 --> 00:09:02.419
if that's not present.

228
00:09:03.500 --> 00:09:04.820
It's the behavior tree component

229
00:09:04.820 --> 00:09:07.620
that's in charge of running that behavior tree

230
00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:09.820
and it doesn't come automatically attached.

231
00:09:09.820 --> 00:09:12.620
So just to kind of actually quickly demonstrate this,

232
00:09:12.620 --> 00:09:20.220
if I were to just make some new AI controller,

233
00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:23.059
make a blueprint that inherits from AI controller,

234
00:09:23.059 --> 00:09:28.190
we could see that no behavior tree exists.

235
00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:30.509
We can very quickly add one ourselves.

236
00:09:32.919 --> 00:09:36.120
Sorry, English spelling a behavior there, my bad.

237
00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:38.039
So yeah, we can just add a behavior tree ourselves

238
00:09:38.039 --> 00:09:42.960
and like I said, this run,

239
00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:45.679
or is it start?

240
00:09:45.679 --> 00:09:47.759
Yeah, start AI won't work

241
00:09:47.759 --> 00:09:50.840
unless there is a behavior tree added,

242
00:09:50.840 --> 00:09:53.120
but the other way we can do that is through this function,

243
00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:55.679
which is run behavior tree,

244
00:09:55.679 --> 00:09:58.960
and you essentially provide a behavior tree to run

245
00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:00.879
and it will automatically create

246
00:10:00.879 --> 00:10:02.840
an appropriate behavior tree component.

247
00:10:02.840 --> 00:10:06.759
So those are the kind of two ways of doing it.

248
00:10:06.759 --> 00:10:07.919
So the behavior tree itself,

249
00:10:07.919 --> 00:10:09.679
we talked a little bit about the component.

250
00:10:09.679 --> 00:10:12.840
The behavior tree is obviously essentially is,

251
00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:14.039
as we said, the brain.

252
00:10:14.039 --> 00:10:15.919
It allows us, it's a way

253
00:10:15.919 --> 00:10:19.240
of structuring decision-making essentially.

254
00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:21.960
It can be paired with blackboards to,

255
00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:25.720
which introduced the concept of memory to make decisions,

256
00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:28.120
gather up information about the world it's in

257
00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:31.320
and make decisions based off that information.

258
00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:34.679
It's essentially built in a hierarchical manner.

259
00:10:34.679 --> 00:10:36.120
It's a set of nodes that control

260
00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:37.240
the flow of decision-making.

261
00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:39.159
So it might, for people who aren't used

262
00:10:39.159 --> 00:10:40.440
to seeing things like behavior trees,

263
00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:42.879
it might look a little bit like a flow diagram.

264
00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:45.960
And when you're creating a behavior tree,

265
00:10:45.960 --> 00:10:47.159
it has its own editor graph.

266
00:10:47.159 --> 00:10:51.879
So when you might already be used to blueprinting,

267
00:10:51.879 --> 00:10:53.000
if you go into a behavior tree,

268
00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:54.200
it's gonna look a little bit different

269
00:10:54.200 --> 00:10:56.639
because as with several systems in Unreal,

270
00:10:56.639 --> 00:10:59.000
it has its own kind of bespoke editor.

271
00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:00.200
The other thing that's worth mentioning

272
00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:02.480
is a lot of behavior tree logic is event-driven.

273
00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:06.200
It's not really on tick, even though it might look like it.

274
00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:08.000
A lot of what the behavior tree is doing

275
00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:10.080
is sitting and waiting for updates

276
00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:14.019
from whatever is the kind of most relevant node.

277
00:11:14.019 --> 00:11:15.340
So this is just an example

278
00:11:15.340 --> 00:11:17.419
of a behavior tree that we've got here.

279
00:11:17.419 --> 00:11:19.980
This is one, if you download the example project,

280
00:11:19.980 --> 00:11:20.940
this is the behavior tree

281
00:11:20.980 --> 00:11:23.740
that we've already got in our fundamentals folder.

282
00:11:23.740 --> 00:11:25.059
If we go to fundamentals behavior tree,

283
00:11:25.059 --> 00:11:27.340
it's just this BT example.

284
00:11:27.340 --> 00:11:30.019
And essentially this is just a very, very simple setup.

285
00:11:30.019 --> 00:11:32.659
In a kind of fully shift game,

286
00:11:32.659 --> 00:11:34.259
it's much more likely that we would have

287
00:11:34.259 --> 00:11:36.940
a far more substantial setup.

288
00:11:36.940 --> 00:11:39.259
We're gonna go into what each of these nodes is

289
00:11:39.259 --> 00:11:44.100
and how they work very briefly in a little bit, sorry.

290
00:11:44.100 --> 00:11:46.100
But kind of very briefly just to talk about it,

291
00:11:46.100 --> 00:11:48.019
what this is ultimately doing

292
00:11:48.059 --> 00:11:51.460
is behavior trees are importantly structured

293
00:11:51.460 --> 00:11:53.620
top to bottom, left to right.

294
00:11:53.620 --> 00:11:55.419
And you can actually see that denoted

295
00:11:55.419 --> 00:11:57.379
by these little numbers on each of these nodes.

296
00:11:57.379 --> 00:11:59.820
You can see no two of them share a number.

297
00:11:59.820 --> 00:12:01.940
This is the order of priority of these nodes.

298
00:12:01.940 --> 00:12:03.139
So as we can see,

299
00:12:03.139 --> 00:12:05.220
we'll go into what each of these nodes actually is

300
00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:07.899
and what they do, but we have our first node here.

301
00:12:07.899 --> 00:12:09.059
So this is zero,

302
00:12:09.059 --> 00:12:11.299
and then this would be the second node considered.

303
00:12:11.299 --> 00:12:13.379
And then we would always get to this sequence.

304
00:12:13.379 --> 00:12:16.059
Again, we'll talk more about what these do, three, four, five.

305
00:12:16.059 --> 00:12:19.100
And then only after all of these are considered,

306
00:12:19.100 --> 00:12:20.340
then we would go over here.

307
00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:24.899
So the left to rightness of these nodes is very important.

308
00:12:24.899 --> 00:12:29.700
An interesting caveat or an interesting just eccentricity

309
00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:32.220
of the behavior tree system to be aware of

310
00:12:32.220 --> 00:12:36.620
is that the way in which the left to rightness

311
00:12:36.620 --> 00:12:37.700
of these nodes is assessed

312
00:12:37.700 --> 00:12:39.700
might not always be completely obvious.

313
00:12:39.700 --> 00:12:42.899
So one might assume that because this node

314
00:12:42.899 --> 00:12:45.980
is now kind of the arrow is pointing more to the left,

315
00:12:45.980 --> 00:12:48.100
this node would be higher priority.

316
00:12:48.100 --> 00:12:49.100
That's actually not the case.

317
00:12:49.100 --> 00:12:50.740
You can see this has still been assigned four

318
00:12:50.740 --> 00:12:52.100
and this has been assigned three.

319
00:12:52.100 --> 00:12:55.259
It's actually the left most side of the node

320
00:12:55.259 --> 00:12:58.460
is used to assess whether or not one node

321
00:12:58.460 --> 00:13:00.539
is higher priority.

322
00:13:00.539 --> 00:13:02.220
It's a strange little quirk of the system,

323
00:13:02.220 --> 00:13:03.779
but just important to be aware of

324
00:13:03.779 --> 00:13:06.860
because if you have particularly long names,

325
00:13:06.860 --> 00:13:08.460
it can actually end up in a situation

326
00:13:08.460 --> 00:13:12.620
where your nodes act in perhaps slightly surprising ways.

327
00:13:12.620 --> 00:13:16.340
So purely because this runEQS query is so wide

328
00:13:16.340 --> 00:13:18.259
because the name is so long,

329
00:13:18.259 --> 00:13:20.340
it's actually ended up being a higher priority.

330
00:13:20.340 --> 00:13:23.259
But as soon as that becomes even slightly more to the right,

331
00:13:23.259 --> 00:13:24.940
you see this now shifts down in priority.

332
00:13:24.940 --> 00:13:28.899
So that's just the left to rightness is preserved here,

333
00:13:28.899 --> 00:13:30.379
but it's just what exactly that means

334
00:13:30.379 --> 00:13:32.299
in the context of particularly long names.

335
00:13:32.299 --> 00:13:35.820
If I just gave this some absurdly long name,

336
00:13:35.820 --> 00:13:38.860
you can overwrite the names of the nodes.

337
00:13:39.860 --> 00:13:44.679
If I just change this to move to some long name

338
00:13:44.679 --> 00:13:49.220
and I just add a load of ease.

339
00:13:49.220 --> 00:13:50.820
Well, that's shifted it all to the right.

340
00:13:50.820 --> 00:13:52.779
But either way, you might want to centralize that, right?

341
00:13:52.779 --> 00:13:54.740
That might seem like it makes sense,

342
00:13:54.740 --> 00:14:03.840
but now suddenly, if I were to save this.

343
00:14:03.840 --> 00:14:05.320
Okay, well, for some reason,

344
00:14:06.440 --> 00:14:09.120
maybe it doesn't actually read the override name

345
00:14:09.120 --> 00:14:10.759
that you choose, but I've definitely had this before

346
00:14:10.759 --> 00:14:12.759
when I've had long function names

347
00:14:12.799 --> 00:14:15.440
that it has...

348
00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:17.639
Oh no, yeah, that is done exactly as we expected.

349
00:14:17.639 --> 00:14:19.720
Sorry, no, I misread the...

350
00:14:19.720 --> 00:14:22.360
But as you can see, we've got a very long name now

351
00:14:22.360 --> 00:14:25.279
and this is now node three and this is node four

352
00:14:25.279 --> 00:14:28.759
because the left side has been pushed so far to the side.

353
00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:32.440
So just a little quirk that's worth bearing in mind.

354
00:14:36.440 --> 00:14:38.600
But yeah, generally what this is doing

355
00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:42.679
is we can assume that the kind of leftmost behavior

356
00:14:42.679 --> 00:14:43.919
is the highest priority behavior

357
00:14:43.960 --> 00:14:46.120
and this is the kind of fallback behavior.

358
00:14:46.120 --> 00:14:49.240
We've got a behavior tree that's essentially

359
00:14:49.240 --> 00:14:50.399
choosing to do some behavior.

360
00:14:50.399 --> 00:14:52.039
If we have a target,

361
00:14:52.039 --> 00:14:53.919
then we try and find a point around the target.

362
00:14:53.919 --> 00:14:55.720
We move to them and then we attack them.

363
00:14:55.720 --> 00:14:58.679
And if we don't, so this is the higher priority.

364
00:14:58.679 --> 00:15:00.080
If we have a target,

365
00:15:00.080 --> 00:15:02.159
we'll go into how we're achieving that in a little bit.

366
00:15:02.159 --> 00:15:03.519
And then the fallback behavior

367
00:15:03.519 --> 00:15:05.879
is we just find some location around us,

368
00:15:05.879 --> 00:15:06.799
we wait for five seconds

369
00:15:06.799 --> 00:15:09.399
and then we move to that location and just repeat that.

370
00:15:09.399 --> 00:15:11.519
So that's what this behavior tree is doing.

371
00:15:11.559 --> 00:15:13.360
It's relatively simple.

372
00:15:13.360 --> 00:15:15.639
Again, in a kind of full release game,

373
00:15:15.639 --> 00:15:17.480
you would expect to see quite a lot

374
00:15:17.480 --> 00:15:19.120
more sizable behavior trees,

375
00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:22.440
but this is just introducing some simple concepts,

376
00:15:22.440 --> 00:15:24.519
the general structure that behavior tree might have

377
00:15:24.519 --> 00:15:25.600
and just to kind of demonstrate

378
00:15:25.600 --> 00:15:32.779
how each of these nodes works.

379
00:15:32.779 --> 00:15:34.980
So we've already kind of taken a little look

380
00:15:34.980 --> 00:15:36.500
at some of these nodes,

381
00:15:36.500 --> 00:15:38.340
but the actual behavior tree is made up

382
00:15:38.340 --> 00:15:40.659
of five key groups of nodes.

383
00:15:40.659 --> 00:15:42.779
We have the root node, composite nodes

384
00:15:42.779 --> 00:15:44.779
with which there are three different types,

385
00:15:44.779 --> 00:15:47.220
and then task nodes, decorators, and surfaces.

386
00:15:47.220 --> 00:15:49.889
And we'll go into each of those in more detail now.

387
00:15:49.889 --> 00:15:52.850
So the root node, this is just our start point.

388
00:15:52.850 --> 00:15:54.250
It can't be deleted.

389
00:15:54.250 --> 00:15:57.129
The root node can only have one connection.

390
00:15:57.129 --> 00:15:59.009
And if the connection from the root node

391
00:15:59.009 --> 00:16:01.570
to any other nodes is disconnected,

392
00:16:01.570 --> 00:16:04.769
then essentially the entire behavior tree is disabled.

393
00:16:04.769 --> 00:16:06.529
We'll talk more about what decorators

394
00:16:06.529 --> 00:16:08.730
and surfaces are in a second,

395
00:16:08.730 --> 00:16:11.169
but the root node can't have decorators

396
00:16:11.169 --> 00:16:12.610
or surfaces attached to it.

397
00:16:12.610 --> 00:16:14.889
And it's kind of got no inherent functionality.

398
00:16:14.889 --> 00:16:16.610
It's really just an entry point.

399
00:16:16.610 --> 00:16:19.090
And if you are to select the root node,

400
00:16:19.090 --> 00:16:20.210
really all you're doing

401
00:16:20.210 --> 00:16:22.129
is talking about the behavior tree as a whole.

402
00:16:22.129 --> 00:16:24.649
So the kind of default behavior,

403
00:16:24.649 --> 00:16:26.129
whereas with most of the nodes,

404
00:16:26.129 --> 00:16:28.049
you can kind of set properties about them.

405
00:16:28.049 --> 00:16:31.649
You can specify bits of information about that node itself.

406
00:16:31.649 --> 00:16:34.049
The root node, all you do is no different

407
00:16:34.049 --> 00:16:37.490
to kind of clicking off in the behavior tree in general.

408
00:16:37.490 --> 00:16:40.570
All you do is assign the blackboard,

409
00:16:40.570 --> 00:16:41.409
but that is kind of,

410
00:16:41.409 --> 00:16:42.610
that's nothing to do with the root.

411
00:16:42.610 --> 00:16:43.929
That's the behavior tree itself.

412
00:16:43.929 --> 00:16:45.450
It's just to say that the root node

413
00:16:45.450 --> 00:16:52.639
kind of has no extra information that you can set.

414
00:16:52.639 --> 00:16:54.600
So then we have composite nodes.

415
00:16:54.600 --> 00:16:57.240
Composite nodes essentially define our,

416
00:16:57.240 --> 00:17:00.600
kind of a branch in our behavior tree's logic.

417
00:17:00.600 --> 00:17:02.840
And each different kind of composite node

418
00:17:02.840 --> 00:17:05.839
determines rules for how that branch is executed.

419
00:17:05.839 --> 00:17:08.319
Only composite nodes can be attached to the root node.

420
00:17:08.319 --> 00:17:10.319
So tasks, decorators, surfaces

421
00:17:10.319 --> 00:17:13.119
can't be utilized directly off the root node.

422
00:17:13.480 --> 00:17:16.559
The first node that your root node enters into

423
00:17:16.559 --> 00:17:18.400
needs to be a composite node.

424
00:17:18.400 --> 00:17:20.559
And composite node, unlike the root node,

425
00:17:20.559 --> 00:17:23.099
do support both decorators and surfaces.

426
00:17:24.599 --> 00:17:26.240
As you said, there are three types of competent nodes.

427
00:17:26.240 --> 00:17:28.440
We have selectors, sequences, and simple parallels,

428
00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:31.339
and we'll kind of go into those in more detail now.

429
00:17:31.339 --> 00:17:33.740
So first off, we have selectors.

430
00:17:33.740 --> 00:17:35.700
Selectors essentially,

431
00:17:37.140 --> 00:17:40.660
kind of two, we've talked about kind of priority,

432
00:17:40.660 --> 00:17:43.299
but the other important concept to understand

433
00:17:43.299 --> 00:17:45.579
in behavior trees is there is this concept

434
00:17:45.579 --> 00:17:47.420
of success and failure.

435
00:17:47.420 --> 00:17:50.019
Nodes could really have three states.

436
00:17:50.019 --> 00:17:52.299
They have, they can succeed, they can fail,

437
00:17:52.299 --> 00:17:53.579
or they can be kind of pending.

438
00:17:53.579 --> 00:17:56.299
They can be, you know, not finished yet.

439
00:17:56.299 --> 00:17:59.299
But ultimately, once a node concludes,

440
00:17:59.299 --> 00:18:01.420
it will either succeed or fail.

441
00:18:01.420 --> 00:18:04.380
So the purposes of selectors and sequences

442
00:18:04.380 --> 00:18:06.019
is respond in different ways

443
00:18:06.019 --> 00:18:07.960
to this idea of success and failure.

444
00:18:07.960 --> 00:18:10.920
The purpose of selector, as kind of implied by the name,

445
00:18:10.920 --> 00:18:15.319
is there to select a particular behavior.

446
00:18:15.319 --> 00:18:18.039
So a selector can have multiple children,

447
00:18:18.039 --> 00:18:19.839
and what the selector is there to do

448
00:18:19.839 --> 00:18:23.559
is find the first successful child.

449
00:18:23.559 --> 00:18:25.059
So it goes from left to right,

450
00:18:25.059 --> 00:18:27.039
as with all behavior trees by default.

451
00:18:27.039 --> 00:18:28.079
It will go from left to right

452
00:18:28.079 --> 00:18:29.279
through each of the children

453
00:18:29.279 --> 00:18:32.359
and find the first child that succeeds

454
00:18:32.359 --> 00:18:33.880
and then return itself.

455
00:18:33.880 --> 00:18:36.400
So a selector, you enter the selector,

456
00:18:36.400 --> 00:18:39.400
the selector goes from left, assesses the first child.

457
00:18:39.400 --> 00:18:40.819
If that child succeeds,

458
00:18:40.819 --> 00:18:44.119
the selector itself will instantly succeed

459
00:18:44.119 --> 00:18:44.960
and exit out.

460
00:18:44.960 --> 00:18:47.200
And then, you know, if the flow returns to the selector,

461
00:18:47.200 --> 00:18:49.519
it will then start from the beginning again.

462
00:18:49.519 --> 00:18:52.279
If the first child fails,

463
00:18:52.279 --> 00:18:54.019
then it will move on to the next child

464
00:18:54.019 --> 00:18:56.119
and ask that child if it succeeds or fail.

465
00:18:56.119 --> 00:18:59.359
And the selector will keep going left to the right

466
00:18:59.359 --> 00:19:01.680
as long as the children keep failing

467
00:19:01.680 --> 00:19:03.079
until it gets to the last child.

468
00:19:03.079 --> 00:19:05.319
And if the last child fails,

469
00:19:05.339 --> 00:19:06.980
then the entire selector fails.

470
00:19:06.980 --> 00:19:09.839
So as well as the children having concepts

471
00:19:09.839 --> 00:19:11.740
of failing and succeeding,

472
00:19:11.740 --> 00:19:15.240
the selector itself has a concept of failing and succeeding.

473
00:19:15.240 --> 00:19:17.920
The selector fails if all of its children fail.

474
00:19:17.920 --> 00:19:21.779
As soon as the selector finds one child that succeeds,

475
00:19:21.779 --> 00:19:24.240
the selector itself succeeds and exits out.

476
00:19:26.250 --> 00:19:28.630
The sequence is kind of the opposite, the inversion.

477
00:19:28.630 --> 00:19:32.690
So where the selector is trying to find a single node

478
00:19:32.690 --> 00:19:33.890
that succeeds,

479
00:19:33.910 --> 00:19:37.009
the sequence is trying to,

480
00:19:37.009 --> 00:19:40.609
essentially is intending to execute all of its children

481
00:19:40.609 --> 00:19:43.470
and will stop as soon as it hits one that fails.

482
00:19:43.470 --> 00:19:46.849
So whereas the selector expects one succeeding child,

483
00:19:46.849 --> 00:19:48.549
the sequencer kind of hopes,

484
00:19:48.549 --> 00:19:51.690
as much as, you know, code desires anything,

485
00:19:51.690 --> 00:19:54.549
for all of its children to succeed,

486
00:19:54.549 --> 00:19:57.049
as I'm sure we all do in life.

487
00:19:57.049 --> 00:19:59.289
So the same as selector,

488
00:19:59.289 --> 00:20:02.289
the same as all behavior trees run left to right,

489
00:20:02.329 --> 00:20:05.730
a sequence will execute its leftmost child first.

490
00:20:05.730 --> 00:20:07.529
If that child fails,

491
00:20:07.529 --> 00:20:11.069
the sequence will instantly itself fail and exit out.

492
00:20:12.069 --> 00:20:13.410
If that child succeeds,

493
00:20:13.410 --> 00:20:15.289
it moves to the right onto its next child

494
00:20:15.289 --> 00:20:18.309
and will proceed and will keep executing the next child

495
00:20:18.309 --> 00:20:20.329
when the previous child succeeds.

496
00:20:20.329 --> 00:20:22.049
If the previous child succeeds,

497
00:20:22.049 --> 00:20:23.849
as soon as one of them fails,

498
00:20:23.849 --> 00:20:28.849
it will exit out of the behavior and continue onwards.

499
00:20:30.220 --> 00:20:32.200
And then lastly, we have simple parallel.

500
00:20:32.200 --> 00:20:35.759
This is less for controlling flow and more just

501
00:20:35.759 --> 00:20:37.960
it's kind of useful bit of functionality.

502
00:20:38.880 --> 00:20:43.880
Essentially, we have a kind of main task

503
00:20:43.960 --> 00:20:46.200
that the parallel executes.

504
00:20:46.200 --> 00:20:49.640
It's important to mention that what the parallel,

505
00:20:49.640 --> 00:20:51.880
the kind of parallel node does,

506
00:20:51.880 --> 00:20:55.079
is it executes a tree whilst performing a task.

507
00:20:55.079 --> 00:20:58.700
So the task itself must be a task node,

508
00:20:58.700 --> 00:21:00.839
which again, we'll talk about next.

509
00:21:00.839 --> 00:21:05.079
And then what it expects from the kind of second branch

510
00:21:05.079 --> 00:21:07.680
is likely going to be a composite node.

511
00:21:07.680 --> 00:21:10.039
You could maybe plug a task into that as well.

512
00:21:10.039 --> 00:21:11.660
I've never tried that myself,

513
00:21:11.660 --> 00:21:15.079
but the typical plan would be to have a, you know,

514
00:21:15.079 --> 00:21:18.119
an entire kind of other branch of your tree.

515
00:21:18.119 --> 00:21:19.799
And that branch is executed

516
00:21:19.799 --> 00:21:23.480
whilst the primary task is being executed.

517
00:21:23.480 --> 00:21:27.119
An example of this might be you have some kind of

518
00:21:28.119 --> 00:21:31.759
look at player logic that you want to execute

519
00:21:31.759 --> 00:21:35.000
whilst attacking the player or something like that.

520
00:21:35.000 --> 00:21:38.119
So you can perform actions in the background

521
00:21:38.119 --> 00:21:40.059
whilst you're doing a certain thing.

522
00:21:40.059 --> 00:21:41.240
The other thing that you have

523
00:21:41.240 --> 00:21:43.480
is this finish mode variable.

524
00:21:43.480 --> 00:21:47.920
You can, if I were to just quickly add in a parallel node,

525
00:21:47.920 --> 00:21:49.000
I don't think I've got one here,

526
00:21:49.000 --> 00:21:50.480
but if I add in a parallel,

527
00:21:50.480 --> 00:21:53.720
you can see you've got a couple of different finish modes.

528
00:21:53.720 --> 00:21:56.000
You have immediate and delayed.

529
00:21:56.039 --> 00:21:58.839
Immediate essentially means as soon as the task finishes,

530
00:21:58.839 --> 00:22:02.200
the tree that is underneath it gets aborted.

531
00:22:02.200 --> 00:22:05.400
So it stops executing regardless of what it's doing.

532
00:22:05.400 --> 00:22:08.799
Otherwise we have delayed where when the task,

533
00:22:08.799 --> 00:22:10.200
whatever task is plugged in here,

534
00:22:10.200 --> 00:22:12.480
finishes, concludes, succeeds, or fails,

535
00:22:13.319 --> 00:22:16.720
the tree will get to its own natural finish state

536
00:22:16.720 --> 00:22:20.849
before then exiting out.

537
00:22:20.849 --> 00:22:23.089
So that's all of our composite nodes.

538
00:22:23.089 --> 00:22:25.150
Next, we have task nodes.

539
00:22:25.150 --> 00:22:27.369
Task nodes are essentially the nodes that do things.

540
00:22:27.529 --> 00:22:29.390
It's again, implied by the name.

541
00:22:30.410 --> 00:22:31.529
And they essentially,

542
00:22:31.529 --> 00:22:34.490
this is like a little bit like our function call.

543
00:22:34.490 --> 00:22:36.049
They're the things that do things.

544
00:22:36.049 --> 00:22:38.950
The majority of a behavior tree

545
00:22:38.950 --> 00:22:41.009
is likely to be made up of task nodes.

546
00:22:42.170 --> 00:22:45.930
And task nodes themselves support decorators and services

547
00:22:45.930 --> 00:22:47.730
and are obviously intended to be placed

548
00:22:47.730 --> 00:22:49.730
on the behavior tree graph.

549
00:22:49.730 --> 00:22:52.089
There's a couple of preexisting task nodes,

550
00:22:52.089 --> 00:22:54.930
but you can also make your own in Blueprint,

551
00:22:54.930 --> 00:22:58.000
which we'll talk about a little bit more in a bit.

552
00:22:58.000 --> 00:23:01.000
But just some examples of kind of preexisting ones

553
00:23:01.000 --> 00:23:02.359
is if you just look under,

554
00:23:02.359 --> 00:23:03.759
if you right click inside a behavior tree

555
00:23:03.759 --> 00:23:04.680
and you've got tasks,

556
00:23:04.680 --> 00:23:06.079
these are the ones we've made ourselves,

557
00:23:06.079 --> 00:23:07.960
but you can see the kind of things

558
00:23:07.960 --> 00:23:09.339
that you might already have available to you

559
00:23:09.339 --> 00:23:10.180
in the engine.

560
00:23:10.180 --> 00:23:11.799
Move to being a very, very common one

561
00:23:11.799 --> 00:23:14.240
that's obviously using the nav system,

562
00:23:14.240 --> 00:23:16.880
playing sounds, playing animations,

563
00:23:16.880 --> 00:23:18.440
running an EQS query.

564
00:23:18.440 --> 00:23:19.660
You can see the kinds of things

565
00:23:19.660 --> 00:23:23.410
that you might use a task for.

566
00:23:23.410 --> 00:23:25.410
Next, we have decorators.

567
00:23:25.410 --> 00:23:27.369
As has kind of already been mentioned,

568
00:23:27.369 --> 00:23:30.650
decorators can be added to either composites or task nodes.

569
00:23:30.650 --> 00:23:34.170
And decorators essentially give us a way

570
00:23:34.170 --> 00:23:39.170
to inject branching kind of conditions onto our logic.

571
00:23:40.589 --> 00:23:42.450
So yeah, other behavior trees,

572
00:23:42.450 --> 00:23:47.170
obviously behavior trees aren't a unique to Unreal.

573
00:23:47.170 --> 00:23:49.970
They have their own kind of like bespoke implementation,

574
00:23:49.970 --> 00:23:51.450
but obviously behavior trees are a concept

575
00:23:51.450 --> 00:23:53.569
that exists outside of Unreal as a whole.

576
00:23:53.569 --> 00:23:56.769
So the terminology here might be slightly different,

577
00:23:56.769 --> 00:23:58.809
but I believe these would be called conditionals

578
00:23:58.809 --> 00:24:01.289
in other behavior tree systems.

579
00:24:01.289 --> 00:24:04.630
So essentially we can add decorators to other nodes,

580
00:24:04.630 --> 00:24:07.990
be that composite nodes or task nodes,

581
00:24:07.990 --> 00:24:11.170
and they give us a way to kind of affect

582
00:24:11.170 --> 00:24:14.490
the context in which that node will actually execute.

583
00:24:15.829 --> 00:24:17.589
And in certain contexts,

584
00:24:17.589 --> 00:24:21.630
depending on the kind of decorator that you've got applied,

585
00:24:21.630 --> 00:24:23.789
you can actually even use that decorator

586
00:24:24.549 --> 00:24:26.509
to abort out of other parts of the tree.

587
00:24:26.509 --> 00:24:27.990
We said it before about this idea

588
00:24:27.990 --> 00:24:31.670
of behavior trees being event-based.

589
00:24:31.670 --> 00:24:33.589
Some of that event-based behavior

590
00:24:33.589 --> 00:24:35.890
really comes out in this idea

591
00:24:35.890 --> 00:24:38.829
that we kind of listen to changes within our,

592
00:24:38.829 --> 00:24:42.190
or we can listen to changes within our decorators

593
00:24:42.190 --> 00:24:44.430
for when that decorator state changes,

594
00:24:44.430 --> 00:24:46.730
we can instantly alter our behavior.

595
00:24:48.390 --> 00:24:51.750
So particularly in the context of a decorator,

596
00:24:51.750 --> 00:24:54.269
this is an example of one that we've used in this tree

597
00:24:54.269 --> 00:24:55.470
where we've got a decorator

598
00:24:55.470 --> 00:24:58.589
that is listening to a blackboard key.

599
00:24:58.589 --> 00:25:01.750
We'll talk more about blackboard keys in a little bit,

600
00:25:01.750 --> 00:25:04.950
but essentially just imagine this is a variable

601
00:25:04.950 --> 00:25:06.869
and this variable can change.

602
00:25:06.869 --> 00:25:09.309
And this is an if saying

603
00:25:09.309 --> 00:25:11.710
that we don't want to perform all of this logic

604
00:25:11.710 --> 00:25:15.349
if this variable doesn't have a valid value in it.

605
00:25:15.349 --> 00:25:16.269
But as well as that,

606
00:25:16.269 --> 00:25:19.710
we have the ability to kind of abort parts of the tree.

607
00:25:19.710 --> 00:25:20.990
So as you can see here,

608
00:25:21.029 --> 00:25:25.869
this isn't true for all blackboards, for all decorators,

609
00:25:25.869 --> 00:25:27.509
but for some of them,

610
00:25:27.509 --> 00:25:30.630
particularly blackboard-based decorators,

611
00:25:30.630 --> 00:25:33.470
we have this option for kind of aborting.

612
00:25:33.470 --> 00:25:34.309
And you can kind of see

613
00:25:34.309 --> 00:25:35.589
we've got these different options here.

614
00:25:35.589 --> 00:25:37.930
We can not abort at all.

615
00:25:37.930 --> 00:25:42.390
We can abort just the tree itself.

616
00:25:42.390 --> 00:25:46.109
We can abort any part of the behavior tree

617
00:25:46.109 --> 00:25:48.430
that is lower priority than this node.

618
00:25:48.430 --> 00:25:49.390
And we can do both.

619
00:25:49.390 --> 00:25:52.509
We can abort this part of the tree itself

620
00:25:52.509 --> 00:25:55.140
and everything that is lower priority.

621
00:25:55.140 --> 00:25:57.940
And the two situations which we can abort

622
00:25:57.940 --> 00:25:59.980
is when the result has changed.

623
00:25:59.980 --> 00:26:02.420
So this depends on what query

624
00:26:02.420 --> 00:26:04.660
are we asking about this variable

625
00:26:04.660 --> 00:26:07.180
and has the output of that query changed?

626
00:26:07.180 --> 00:26:08.500
Is it true or false?

627
00:26:08.500 --> 00:26:11.140
And the other situation in which we can react to that

628
00:26:11.140 --> 00:26:12.400
is when the value itself,

629
00:26:12.400 --> 00:26:14.539
so the data being plugged in,

630
00:26:14.539 --> 00:26:17.819
our actual, the value inside our variable has changed

631
00:26:17.819 --> 00:26:19.099
regardless of whether or not

632
00:26:19.099 --> 00:26:20.779
we're going to react to it in a different way,

633
00:26:20.779 --> 00:26:23.140
regardless of whether our output is true or false,

634
00:26:23.140 --> 00:26:25.740
just because the input data has changed,

635
00:26:25.740 --> 00:26:28.640
we want to possibly abort in that situation.

636
00:26:28.640 --> 00:26:31.240
So it gives us this kind of very powerful logic

637
00:26:31.240 --> 00:26:34.579
to react to things more instantaneously

638
00:26:35.740 --> 00:26:37.099
only in certain situations,

639
00:26:37.099 --> 00:26:40.640
only in certain priority levels.

640
00:26:40.640 --> 00:26:42.099
So we'll just change that back to,

641
00:26:42.099 --> 00:26:44.660
I think this was set to kind of lower priority.

642
00:26:46.279 --> 00:26:47.579
So that's decorators.

643
00:26:47.579 --> 00:26:49.779
And then lastly, we have service nodes.

644
00:26:49.779 --> 00:26:52.819
Service nodes are essentially give us a way to implement

645
00:26:54.710 --> 00:26:57.789
almost in the background behavior.

646
00:26:58.730 --> 00:27:01.150
They essentially execute,

647
00:27:01.150 --> 00:27:03.109
they are a kind of node that you attach,

648
00:27:03.109 --> 00:27:06.190
as we already said, to either composites or tasks,

649
00:27:06.190 --> 00:27:10.190
and they will execute on a defined frequency

650
00:27:10.190 --> 00:27:12.230
as long as the node that they are attached to

651
00:27:12.230 --> 00:27:14.509
or the part of the branch that they are attached to

652
00:27:14.509 --> 00:27:16.089
is being executed.

653
00:27:16.089 --> 00:27:21.089
So we've got an example in the behavior tree here.

654
00:27:21.430 --> 00:27:25.230
We've got a, this is a very, very common one.

655
00:27:25.230 --> 00:27:29.869
I think the only default engine service

656
00:27:29.869 --> 00:27:31.490
is to run an EQS.

657
00:27:31.490 --> 00:27:33.289
This EQS actually isn't doing anything,

658
00:27:33.289 --> 00:27:35.650
but it's just kind of there to demonstrate its use.

659
00:27:35.650 --> 00:27:37.690
But essentially as long as we are within

660
00:27:37.690 --> 00:27:40.490
one of these nodes within this part of the tree,

661
00:27:40.490 --> 00:27:43.569
this EQS will be, this service will be running.

662
00:27:43.569 --> 00:27:46.410
And what it will be doing is on an interval of 0.5 seconds

663
00:27:46.410 --> 00:27:48.529
with a variance of 0.1.

664
00:27:48.529 --> 00:27:52.970
So between every 0.4 to 0.6 seconds,

665
00:27:52.970 --> 00:27:54.809
we will run an EQS query.

666
00:27:54.809 --> 00:27:58.250
We will talk more about what an EQS query actually is

667
00:27:58.250 --> 00:27:59.730
a little bit later, but you know,

668
00:27:59.730 --> 00:28:02.049
this same thing could apply to any logic,

669
00:28:02.049 --> 00:28:04.970
swap EQS query out for whatever logic you want to do.

670
00:28:04.970 --> 00:28:09.450
It could be try and find a possible reload point.

671
00:28:09.450 --> 00:28:12.269
So try and find the most powerful weapon in the map

672
00:28:12.269 --> 00:28:14.990
on a periodic, like every 10 seconds,

673
00:28:14.990 --> 00:28:16.869
just check if the most powerful weapon in the map

674
00:28:16.869 --> 00:28:19.269
has spawned in and then maybe we would want to react

675
00:28:19.269 --> 00:28:20.670
to that logic in some way.

676
00:28:21.890 --> 00:28:26.490
So again, they are essentially time-based logic

677
00:28:26.490 --> 00:28:29.150
that will execute whilst their part of the tree

678
00:28:29.150 --> 00:28:30.529
is being executed.

679
00:28:30.529 --> 00:28:33.210
Obviously, if you were to add a service to a task,

680
00:28:34.230 --> 00:28:36.730
then that service would only execute

681
00:28:36.730 --> 00:28:39.789
whilst that task is being executed.

682
00:28:39.789 --> 00:28:41.670
So it's a little bit like running parallel behavior.

683
00:28:42.309 --> 00:28:45.230
In some ways, the parallel node and service nodes

684
00:28:45.230 --> 00:28:47.430
kind of perform a somewhat similar function.

685
00:28:49.380 --> 00:28:50.619
Yeah, as we already said,

686
00:28:50.619 --> 00:28:53.339
EQS is one of the kind of preexisting ones

687
00:28:53.339 --> 00:28:54.900
that exist in Unreal.

688
00:28:56.420 --> 00:28:58.460
And yeah, it's important to mention

689
00:28:58.460 --> 00:29:02.500
that services don't have to tick.

690
00:29:02.500 --> 00:29:07.440
They can also, they can just be used

691
00:29:07.440 --> 00:29:09.400
for kind of like entry and exit logic

692
00:29:09.400 --> 00:29:10.240
for when their tree,

693
00:29:10.240 --> 00:29:12.259
part of the tree has been entered and exited.

694
00:29:13.220 --> 00:29:15.259
And knowledge of relevancy from networking

695
00:29:15.259 --> 00:29:18.079
can also be kind of leveraged within them.

696
00:29:18.079 --> 00:29:19.740
As with tasks decorators,

697
00:29:19.740 --> 00:29:22.740
and really all of the fundamental parts

698
00:29:22.740 --> 00:29:24.500
of the behavior tree system,

699
00:29:24.500 --> 00:29:27.700
all of this stuff can be created in Blueprint and C++.

700
00:29:29.980 --> 00:29:35.009
So when we're talking about behavior trees in general,

701
00:29:36.130 --> 00:29:37.890
one of the things that's worth mentioning

702
00:29:37.890 --> 00:29:40.490
is the fact that all of these nodes,

703
00:29:40.490 --> 00:29:41.930
when we see a behavior tree,

704
00:29:41.930 --> 00:29:44.210
it might be an expectation

705
00:29:44.210 --> 00:29:48.809
that when we have multiple AIs running this behavior tree,

706
00:29:48.809 --> 00:29:52.450
each AI would end up with a copy of that behavior tree.

707
00:29:52.450 --> 00:29:54.490
And to a degree, they do.

708
00:29:54.490 --> 00:29:56.650
But when you have multiple AIs

709
00:29:56.650 --> 00:29:58.089
using the same behavior tree,

710
00:29:58.089 --> 00:29:59.529
actually the default behavior

711
00:29:59.529 --> 00:30:01.450
is that each of these nodes

712
00:30:01.450 --> 00:30:04.569
is shared between each of the AIs.

713
00:30:04.569 --> 00:30:07.690
So if you have 20 AIs

714
00:30:07.690 --> 00:30:10.369
running this BT example behavior tree,

715
00:30:10.369 --> 00:30:12.690
this selector, this sequence,

716
00:30:12.690 --> 00:30:16.809
this run EQS query would by default all be shared.

717
00:30:16.809 --> 00:30:19.410
So there is actually only one EQS query.

718
00:30:19.410 --> 00:30:20.569
There's only one sequence.

719
00:30:20.569 --> 00:30:24.029
There is only one selector that all of these AIs share.

720
00:30:24.029 --> 00:30:27.599
That is the default implementation for nodes.

721
00:30:27.599 --> 00:30:29.119
Obviously this comes with certain benefits.

722
00:30:29.119 --> 00:30:32.000
It's improved CPU usage, it's improved memory usage.

723
00:30:32.000 --> 00:30:35.180
We just don't need to use or do as much stuff.

724
00:30:35.180 --> 00:30:40.180
But obviously when we want kind of like instanced

725
00:30:40.259 --> 00:30:42.299
or agent-specific data,

726
00:30:43.400 --> 00:30:45.420
that becomes a little bit of a challenge, right?

727
00:30:45.420 --> 00:30:47.599
The fact that all of this information is always shared

728
00:30:47.599 --> 00:30:49.980
between everyone using that behavior tree

729
00:30:49.980 --> 00:30:51.779
has performance advantages,

730
00:30:51.779 --> 00:30:56.440
but it comes at the cost of we have all of the

731
00:30:56.440 --> 00:30:58.700
information that is used across these things is shared.

732
00:30:58.700 --> 00:31:02.059
And so that is kind of undesirable for obvious reasons.

733
00:31:02.059 --> 00:31:04.720
So in comes the various ways

734
00:31:04.720 --> 00:31:07.099
that we can kind of introduce this concept,

735
00:31:07.099 --> 00:31:10.339
introduce ways to provide

736
00:31:10.339 --> 00:31:12.200
that kind of more instance-based logic.

737
00:31:12.200 --> 00:31:16.180
So the first example is we have instancing nodes.

738
00:31:16.180 --> 00:31:18.779
This is essentially for particular nodes

739
00:31:18.779 --> 00:31:20.539
in our behavior tree.

740
00:31:20.539 --> 00:31:23.500
We can introduce the idea that this node,

741
00:31:23.500 --> 00:31:26.059
for every copy of this node, it will be an instance.

742
00:31:26.059 --> 00:31:29.859
So it won't be shared by each person

743
00:31:29.859 --> 00:31:30.980
using this behavior tree.

744
00:31:30.980 --> 00:31:34.660
For every agent using this behavior tree,

745
00:31:34.660 --> 00:31:36.140
you would have a copy of this node.

746
00:31:36.140 --> 00:31:38.230
That's what an instance node does.

747
00:31:38.230 --> 00:31:40.509
This is defined in C++.

748
00:31:40.509 --> 00:31:43.109
I don't believe you can define this in Blueprint,

749
00:31:43.109 --> 00:31:45.029
and you would do it typically in the constructor.

750
00:31:45.029 --> 00:31:48.029
So this is just an example in the,

751
00:31:48.029 --> 00:31:50.230
we'll talk about this specifically in a second,

752
00:31:50.230 --> 00:31:53.609
but from the C++ for the Blueprint base,

753
00:31:53.609 --> 00:31:56.069
which is when you're going to make your own

754
00:31:56.069 --> 00:31:57.309
behavior tree tasks,

755
00:31:57.309 --> 00:31:59.009
you would inherit from Blueprint base,

756
00:31:59.009 --> 00:32:02.029
and you can see this be create node instances set to true.

757
00:32:02.029 --> 00:32:04.390
So there are a couple of other nodes that use this

758
00:32:04.390 --> 00:32:05.789
by default in the engine.

759
00:32:06.309 --> 00:32:07.710
Play animation, run behavior tree dynamic

760
00:32:07.710 --> 00:32:08.990
would also use this.

761
00:32:08.990 --> 00:32:11.930
But essentially whenever you're creating

762
00:32:11.930 --> 00:32:14.549
a behavior tree node that you want to be instanced,

763
00:32:14.549 --> 00:32:15.789
so you don't want to be shared

764
00:32:15.789 --> 00:32:18.009
by all instances of this behavior tree,

765
00:32:18.009 --> 00:32:20.069
you would use, in the constructor,

766
00:32:20.069 --> 00:32:23.190
you would set this be create node instance to true.

767
00:32:23.190 --> 00:32:26.789
And then obviously every agent that uses that node,

768
00:32:26.789 --> 00:32:29.950
even if you use that node multiple times within your tree,

769
00:32:29.950 --> 00:32:31.750
there would be a unique copy

770
00:32:31.750 --> 00:32:34.150
for each of those instances in that tree

771
00:32:34.190 --> 00:32:38.150
for each node, for each agent using that tree.

772
00:32:38.150 --> 00:32:40.130
Obviously this comes with performance

773
00:32:40.130 --> 00:32:41.690
and memory usage costs.

774
00:32:41.690 --> 00:32:43.930
You are essentially duplicating data.

775
00:32:43.930 --> 00:32:45.849
More things need to be performed against.

776
00:32:45.849 --> 00:32:49.809
There are more nodes that the CPU has to consider.

777
00:32:49.809 --> 00:32:52.250
So these obviously has certain performance costs.

778
00:32:52.250 --> 00:32:54.130
So you should really only be using these

779
00:32:54.130 --> 00:32:56.950
when you don't feel there is a way

780
00:32:56.950 --> 00:33:00.089
to kind of achieve this another way.

781
00:33:00.089 --> 00:33:01.890
The other way that you can do this

782
00:33:01.890 --> 00:33:03.329
is through node memory.

783
00:33:03.369 --> 00:33:06.650
So again, this is more of a C plus plus concept.

784
00:33:06.650 --> 00:33:10.109
The node memory isn't really available

785
00:33:10.109 --> 00:33:12.089
to the blueprint end of things,

786
00:33:12.089 --> 00:33:14.609
but it essentially allows us to create

787
00:33:14.609 --> 00:33:16.930
a kind of custom struct or class

788
00:33:16.930 --> 00:33:20.890
that represents an individual node's memory.

789
00:33:20.890 --> 00:33:24.029
And then that memory will automatically be passed around

790
00:33:24.029 --> 00:33:27.650
by functions within the C plus plus,

791
00:33:27.650 --> 00:33:30.410
within native implementation of that node.

792
00:33:30.490 --> 00:33:33.809
And you can access and utilize that memory.

793
00:33:33.809 --> 00:33:36.210
There's a function that essentially defines

794
00:33:36.210 --> 00:33:38.569
how big this block of memory is.

795
00:33:38.569 --> 00:33:40.609
So we'll actually just quickly look at the C plus plus

796
00:33:40.609 --> 00:33:43.130
for this, it's probably the best example.

797
00:33:43.130 --> 00:33:48.130
So if we look at the BT task move two,

798
00:33:55.960 --> 00:34:01.250
and if I were to, wrong node there,

799
00:34:02.700 --> 00:34:04.339
if we were to just jump in here,

800
00:34:05.259 --> 00:34:07.099
there's a couple of nodes that we kind of need

801
00:34:07.099 --> 00:34:07.940
to be aware of.

802
00:34:07.940 --> 00:34:13.070
So first off we have the get instance memory size.

803
00:34:13.670 --> 00:34:15.590
So this is essentially saying,

804
00:34:15.590 --> 00:34:18.429
how big does the block of memory need to be

805
00:34:18.429 --> 00:34:22.949
for whatever memory this node is gonna need?

806
00:34:22.949 --> 00:34:26.630
So obviously this would be done on a case by case basis,

807
00:34:26.630 --> 00:34:28.869
but the typical thing is you would implement

808
00:34:28.869 --> 00:34:32.530
some struct or class that represents the memory

809
00:34:32.530 --> 00:34:34.030
that this node needs.

810
00:34:34.550 --> 00:34:35.789
And then you would fill it with all that data.

811
00:34:35.789 --> 00:34:37.789
And then really, as long as you're not doing anything

812
00:34:37.789 --> 00:34:39.750
kind of like crazy or dynamic,

813
00:34:39.750 --> 00:34:42.429
you can just use size of to return

814
00:34:42.429 --> 00:34:44.710
just whatever the size of that struct or class is.

815
00:34:44.710 --> 00:34:47.510
And then that's all you kind of need to fill in.

816
00:34:47.510 --> 00:34:49.730
Then with all of the functions that this,

817
00:34:51.110 --> 00:34:53.030
well, certainly a lot of the functions

818
00:34:53.030 --> 00:34:55.150
that this node can utilize,

819
00:34:55.150 --> 00:34:59.110
that memory is exposed to that function

820
00:34:59.110 --> 00:35:01.389
through this UN8 pointer.

821
00:35:01.389 --> 00:35:03.469
Obviously this is just a pointer

822
00:35:03.949 --> 00:35:06.110
to the kind of like start point of that memory.

823
00:35:06.110 --> 00:35:07.949
And so you would just need to cast that.

824
00:35:07.949 --> 00:35:09.590
So typically you'll see this in,

825
00:35:09.590 --> 00:35:10.710
this is an example,

826
00:35:10.710 --> 00:35:13.389
the move to node is an existing unreal node

827
00:35:13.389 --> 00:35:15.269
that utilizes this functionality.

828
00:35:15.269 --> 00:35:18.630
But you can see here that we are kind of casting

829
00:35:18.630 --> 00:35:20.710
that memory to our actual struct

830
00:35:20.710 --> 00:35:23.030
and then utilizing the memory from that structure.

831
00:35:23.030 --> 00:35:25.420
This is how you would utilize it.

832
00:35:25.420 --> 00:35:27.380
And the other two functions just to be aware of

833
00:35:27.380 --> 00:35:29.820
is this initialize memory and cleanup memory

834
00:35:29.820 --> 00:35:33.000
where you essentially create your instance

835
00:35:33.000 --> 00:35:34.840
of this struct and you clean up

836
00:35:34.840 --> 00:35:36.000
that instance of that struct.

837
00:35:36.000 --> 00:35:37.800
So those are the kind of things to be aware of

838
00:35:37.800 --> 00:35:39.000
when you're using this.

839
00:35:39.920 --> 00:35:41.820
And as you've already seen,

840
00:35:43.960 --> 00:35:45.480
there are examples in the engine

841
00:35:45.480 --> 00:35:47.659
that already utilize this, you can use for reference.

842
00:35:47.659 --> 00:35:49.760
And the other thing to mention is that this,

843
00:35:49.760 --> 00:35:50.800
whatever this memory is,

844
00:35:50.800 --> 00:35:53.039
it's not part of the U object data system.

845
00:35:53.039 --> 00:35:54.639
So you wouldn't be making a,

846
00:35:54.639 --> 00:35:57.079
if you were to make a struct or a class,

847
00:35:57.079 --> 00:35:59.960
this wouldn't be inheriting from U object.

848
00:35:59.960 --> 00:36:02.480
This is stuff that it needs to be manually

849
00:36:02.960 --> 00:36:05.480
created, managed, and cleaned up.

850
00:36:05.480 --> 00:36:08.079
But as we already saw, we have these initialize memory

851
00:36:08.079 --> 00:36:12.070
and cleanup memory functions specifically for that.

852
00:36:12.070 --> 00:36:13.630
So yeah, these are some examples

853
00:36:13.630 --> 00:36:15.849
of where we might be using those.

854
00:36:17.449 --> 00:36:19.090
I think I've kind of already covered,

855
00:36:19.090 --> 00:36:21.809
we've got our initialization, we've got our cleanup.

856
00:36:21.809 --> 00:36:24.230
This is how we might use it within a task.

857
00:36:24.230 --> 00:36:27.349
So we've got this pointer to our memory here, we cast it.

858
00:36:28.369 --> 00:36:31.369
And then similarly, we've also got here an example

859
00:36:31.369 --> 00:36:33.889
of how even outside of one of those functions

860
00:36:33.889 --> 00:36:37.170
that passes in the kind of memory pointer,

861
00:36:37.170 --> 00:36:40.889
as long as we have access to the task node itself,

862
00:36:40.889 --> 00:36:42.369
you can, well, as long as we have access

863
00:36:42.369 --> 00:36:43.909
to the behavior tree component,

864
00:36:43.909 --> 00:36:46.989
and yeah, we can use this get node memory

865
00:36:46.989 --> 00:36:50.429
and then find the instance containing this node

866
00:36:50.429 --> 00:36:51.769
and then get the memory in that way.

867
00:36:51.769 --> 00:36:54.769
So there are ways outside of the node itself

868
00:36:54.769 --> 00:36:58.360
to actually access this stuff if that's required.

869
00:36:58.360 --> 00:37:00.119
And then the last example,

870
00:37:00.119 --> 00:37:01.960
which is more blueprint facing

871
00:37:01.960 --> 00:37:04.539
and is kind of probably going to be the most common way

872
00:37:04.539 --> 00:37:07.280
to manage this memory is through blackboard storage.

873
00:37:07.280 --> 00:37:08.840
We already kind of mentioned the idea

874
00:37:08.840 --> 00:37:11.840
of the blackboard being the way,

875
00:37:11.840 --> 00:37:13.500
the kind of agent's memory.

876
00:37:13.500 --> 00:37:15.280
And so yeah, blackboard storage

877
00:37:15.280 --> 00:37:16.840
is obviously a way to do that.

878
00:37:18.039 --> 00:37:20.840
Essentially, blackboard storage can be accessed

879
00:37:20.840 --> 00:37:23.800
within any individual node.

880
00:37:23.800 --> 00:37:25.840
You just need to pass in a particular,

881
00:37:25.840 --> 00:37:28.719
you need to expose a particular property.

882
00:37:29.239 --> 00:37:30.480
The variable that you'd be exposing

883
00:37:30.480 --> 00:37:32.320
is called a blackboard key selector.

884
00:37:32.320 --> 00:37:35.239
And what that lets us do is it kind of lets us get

885
00:37:35.239 --> 00:37:40.239
and set data from and to a given blackboard.

886
00:37:40.380 --> 00:37:41.920
The kind of thing to bear in mind

887
00:37:41.920 --> 00:37:43.239
that whenever you are working with this

888
00:37:43.239 --> 00:37:47.159
is it will only support a kind of like subset of types

889
00:37:47.159 --> 00:37:49.480
that are suitable for blackboards,

890
00:37:49.480 --> 00:37:52.900
but that does cover the vast majority of situations.

891
00:37:54.920 --> 00:37:56.400
Yeah, so the blackboard data itself

892
00:37:56.400 --> 00:37:58.480
is really the memory of our AI.

893
00:37:58.480 --> 00:38:01.440
We've covered the way that individual nodes

894
00:38:01.440 --> 00:38:04.440
can kind of have this memory so we can instance them.

895
00:38:04.440 --> 00:38:07.039
The node itself can have memory

896
00:38:07.039 --> 00:38:09.159
on a kind of per node basis.

897
00:38:09.159 --> 00:38:12.039
But if we, generally the blackboard

898
00:38:12.039 --> 00:38:15.320
is where we kind of would handle the agent's

899
00:38:15.320 --> 00:38:17.639
more global memory to be possibly shared

900
00:38:17.639 --> 00:38:19.880
between different nodes.

901
00:38:19.880 --> 00:38:22.900
So yeah, the blackboard data is a kind of agent's memory.

902
00:38:24.400 --> 00:38:26.360
And it is, the blackboard itself

903
00:38:26.800 --> 00:38:28.760
is specifically engineered to work alongside

904
00:38:28.760 --> 00:38:30.239
the behavior tree.

905
00:38:30.239 --> 00:38:31.360
The way that data is stored

906
00:38:31.360 --> 00:38:32.460
is through these blackboard keys,

907
00:38:32.460 --> 00:38:34.400
which we kind of already mentioned.

908
00:38:34.400 --> 00:38:38.239
The number of entries available in the blackboard

909
00:38:38.239 --> 00:38:42.239
used to be 255 as of 5.2.

910
00:38:42.239 --> 00:38:45.440
So this blackboard key type that we've said

911
00:38:45.440 --> 00:38:47.920
it used to essentially store an ID under the hood.

912
00:38:47.920 --> 00:38:50.320
Now the ID used to be a UINT8,

913
00:38:50.320 --> 00:38:54.059
which is a value that can only go from zero to 255.

914
00:38:54.059 --> 00:38:57.179
As of 5.2, that changed to an IN32.

915
00:38:57.179 --> 00:38:58.980
So I suspect it's very likely

916
00:38:58.980 --> 00:39:02.940
that we no longer have this cap of 255 entries.

917
00:39:02.940 --> 00:39:05.980
I imagine that's now whatever the maximum capacity

918
00:39:05.980 --> 00:39:07.900
of an IN32 is.

919
00:39:09.480 --> 00:39:10.820
Don't quote me on that.

920
00:39:10.820 --> 00:39:12.300
It's worth exploring on your own.

921
00:39:12.300 --> 00:39:14.659
When I have tried to make blackboards

922
00:39:14.659 --> 00:39:17.780
that have more than 255 entries in them,

923
00:39:17.780 --> 00:39:19.639
they didn't complain.

924
00:39:19.639 --> 00:39:23.300
But you know, be conscious that that is a change

925
00:39:23.380 --> 00:39:25.219
that has taken place, but I can't,

926
00:39:25.219 --> 00:39:27.400
I am not completely certain of the recipe

927
00:39:27.400 --> 00:39:29.840
that there isn't some kind of other place

928
00:39:29.840 --> 00:39:32.920
that is in some way limiting you to 255 entries.

929
00:39:34.420 --> 00:39:37.860
Ultimately, 255 entries is a lot,

930
00:39:37.860 --> 00:39:40.099
accounting for the fact that blackboards

931
00:39:40.099 --> 00:39:41.860
can't store container types.

932
00:39:41.860 --> 00:39:44.460
So they can't have arrays and maps.

933
00:39:44.460 --> 00:39:46.900
And so if you do need, for some reason,

934
00:39:46.900 --> 00:39:49.539
large quantities of actors,

935
00:39:49.539 --> 00:39:51.260
we'll talk about this in a little bit,

936
00:39:51.260 --> 00:39:53.059
actors or any kind of data,

937
00:39:54.340 --> 00:39:56.579
there might be special ways to construct that

938
00:39:56.579 --> 00:39:59.260
to kind of avoid hitting up against this entry limit

939
00:39:59.260 --> 00:40:00.300
if it does exist.

940
00:40:01.739 --> 00:40:04.099
The other thing to mention about blackboards as well

941
00:40:04.099 --> 00:40:06.099
is that blackboards can inherit from each other.

942
00:40:06.099 --> 00:40:07.420
So you can introduce a blackboard

943
00:40:07.420 --> 00:40:09.579
that introduces types A, B, and C,

944
00:40:09.579 --> 00:40:10.820
inherit from that blackboard,

945
00:40:10.820 --> 00:40:15.019
and then you'll automatically get those blackboard keys

946
00:40:15.019 --> 00:40:18.960
introduced to any child blackboards.

947
00:40:18.960 --> 00:40:20.800
So we talked about the kind of like

948
00:40:20.840 --> 00:40:22.559
smaller subset of types

949
00:40:22.559 --> 00:40:25.079
that would be suitable for blackboards.

950
00:40:25.079 --> 00:40:28.440
This would include vector, rotators,

951
00:40:28.440 --> 00:40:30.840
bools, int32s, floats,

952
00:40:30.840 --> 00:40:33.280
a kind of lot of these standard atomic types.

953
00:40:33.280 --> 00:40:36.719
We can also implement U classes, U objects,

954
00:40:36.719 --> 00:40:40.440
so the actual class type itself,

955
00:40:40.440 --> 00:40:42.880
objects, so instances of objects,

956
00:40:43.719 --> 00:40:47.639
F names, enums, strings, structs.

957
00:40:47.639 --> 00:40:49.320
With these kind of specialized types,

958
00:40:49.360 --> 00:40:53.360
like objects and structs and enums,

959
00:40:53.360 --> 00:40:55.199
it's not just a kind of generic,

960
00:40:55.199 --> 00:40:58.320
broad concept of an enum or a struct.

961
00:40:58.320 --> 00:41:02.280
You do actually have the ability to specify

962
00:41:02.280 --> 00:41:03.760
kind of more information about that.

963
00:41:03.760 --> 00:41:05.800
So if you were to specify an object,

964
00:41:05.800 --> 00:41:06.880
you can actually say,

965
00:41:06.880 --> 00:41:09.519
hey, is there a particular class

966
00:41:09.519 --> 00:41:12.079
that this should be inheriting from?

967
00:41:12.079 --> 00:41:13.960
The default would probably be actor.

968
00:41:15.039 --> 00:41:16.960
But also the same thing for an enum.

969
00:41:16.960 --> 00:41:18.599
If you just say this is an enum key,

970
00:41:18.719 --> 00:41:20.320
you can also specify,

971
00:41:20.320 --> 00:41:22.960
is there a particular enum type that this should be?

972
00:41:22.960 --> 00:41:26.360
And lastly, if you went to struct,

973
00:41:26.360 --> 00:41:28.039
similarly, you can specify

974
00:41:29.440 --> 00:41:32.840
what kind of struct should this be using?

975
00:41:32.840 --> 00:41:37.599
So it is not purely just the abstract concept of an enum.

976
00:41:37.599 --> 00:41:39.480
It can be a kind of typed enum.

977
00:41:41.079 --> 00:41:45.360
The other thing to mention is that since Unreal 5.5,

978
00:41:45.360 --> 00:41:47.519
there is also the idea of instance structs.

979
00:41:47.519 --> 00:41:49.679
This is not a talk we're gonna kind of go into those,

980
00:41:49.679 --> 00:41:51.880
but if you're pre-5.5,

981
00:41:51.880 --> 00:41:55.400
I'm not sure that same type will be available to you,

982
00:41:55.400 --> 00:41:57.440
but instance structs are essentially structs

983
00:41:57.440 --> 00:41:59.320
that behave a little bit more like objects

984
00:41:59.320 --> 00:42:01.400
in that they occupy

985
00:42:03.360 --> 00:42:04.719
location in memory on the heap,

986
00:42:04.719 --> 00:42:06.519
and that memory can be shared

987
00:42:06.519 --> 00:42:08.639
between multiple instances of the struct,

988
00:42:08.639 --> 00:42:11.239
giving you that kind of nice editor

989
00:42:12.760 --> 00:42:15.000
editability of a struct,

990
00:42:15.000 --> 00:42:16.760
but with the idea that when you're passing

991
00:42:16.760 --> 00:42:17.880
this struct around,

992
00:42:17.880 --> 00:42:19.880
you're actually passing around a pointer

993
00:42:19.880 --> 00:42:22.360
to a location in memory that is shared.

994
00:42:22.360 --> 00:42:24.360
Worth looking into in your own time,

995
00:42:24.360 --> 00:42:25.960
but just worth mentioning that instance structs

996
00:42:25.960 --> 00:42:28.599
have been introduced as a blackboard type.

997
00:42:28.599 --> 00:42:29.559
For all the types,

998
00:42:29.559 --> 00:42:31.440
it's just worth looking at the blackboard key

999
00:42:31.440 --> 00:42:32.840
all types.header,

1000
00:42:34.000 --> 00:42:36.000
just to kind of see all the types available to you,

1001
00:42:36.000 --> 00:42:37.199
but this is kind of like

1002
00:42:37.199 --> 00:42:40.519
most of the types you would typically be using.

1003
00:42:40.519 --> 00:42:42.719
And as I've already mentioned,

1004
00:42:42.719 --> 00:42:44.239
container types aren't supported.

1005
00:42:44.239 --> 00:42:47.679
So maps, arrays, none of these types would be available.
